# Patch: MaxVio load-balance tracking and the OLMoE expert-specialization analysis
#
# Goal:
#   Compare MoE training methods on (a) how balanced their routing is and (b) how specialized
#   their experts end up, not just on loss.
#
# What this patch does:
#   1. megatron/core/transformer/moe/routing_stats.py (new): TopKRouter.routing() hands every
#      routing decision to routing_stats.observe(). It is monitor-only (never touches probs,
#      routing_map or gradients) and uses the PRE-capacity-drop assignment the router actually
#      chose, including expert bias (aux-free LB) and bandit exploration. (The expert_imbalance
#      and load/* metrics of patches 0003/0004 recompute top-k from the raw logits and therefore
#      miss both; do not use them to compare aux-free or bandit arms.)
#        - training step: per-layer assignment fractions -> tracker entry "maxvio" (reduce_op
#          "maxvio") and, with a capacity factor, "moe/token_drop_fraction";
#        - recording mode: each token's top-k expert indices per layer (analysis forwards);
#        - other no-grad forwards (validation): per-layer assignment counts.
#   2. moe_utils.py: reduce_op "maxvio" stores the per-layer assignment vector, all-reduces it
#      over the data-parallel group at logging time (= the whole global batch) and logs
#      MaxVio_batch = (max_i load_i - mean load) / mean load (arXiv 2408.15664) as
#      maxvio/batch (mean over layers, as in the paper), maxvio/batch_worst_layer and
#      maxvio/batch_layer_XX.
#   3. megatron/training/routing_analysis.py (new), called from training.py:
#        - after every validation: MaxVio_global over the validation set -> maxvio/global_valid*;
#        - after every in-loop evaluation: routes a fixed probe set (--routing-probe-seqs per
#          domain) and keeps the top-k indices (router-saturation snapshot);
#        - after training: routes the whole domain-labelled set (--routing-analysis-data, built
#          by lm/data/build_domain_eval.py) and computes the OLMoE (arXiv 2409.02060) metrics:
#            domain specialization  N^(k)_{Ei,D} / N_D (uniform = k/E), plus the normalized
#                                   mutual information I(domain; expert) / H(domain);
#            vocabulary spec.       per expert, the mean over its routed tokens of the share of
#                                   that token type's assignments going to it (uniform = k/E),
#                                   for input tokens and predicted tokens, with top tokens;
#            co-activation          N_{Ei,Ej} / N_{Ei}; summary = mean_i max_{j!=i};
#            router saturation      mean |E_t ∩ E_T| / k per snapshot vs the final model;
#          plus collapse indicators (dead experts < 10% of uniform load, effective number of
#          experts exp(H(load)), MaxVio on the domain set) and per-domain perplexity. Written to
#          <--routing-analysis-out>/specialization.json, logged to W&B under spec/* (scalars,
#          tables, heatmaps) and the JSON uploaded to the run.
#        - --save-final-weights: plain torch.save of the final state dict (Megatron
#          checkpointing is disabled on GH200 due to a SIGSEGV), for offline re-analysis.
#
# Scope / caveats:
#   Assumes TP=CP=PP=EP=1 (data-parallel only), which is what launch.sh runs.
#
# How to find the right location in a future Megatron version:
#   router.py: TopKRouter.routing(), around apply_router_token_dropping.
#   moe_utils.py: save_to_aux_losses_tracker / track_moe_metrics.
#   training.py: after train() returns in pretrain(); the in-loop evaluate_and_print_results
#   call in train(); after evaluate() in evaluate_and_print_results().
#
# Applied to: Megatron-LM core_v0.16.1 (commit 55ac7082), on top of patches 0001-0006.
#
